
# Google Meet 

Google Meet records videos at a variable frame rate, which can cause playback and editing issues in Blender. Blender works best with constant-frame-rate video, where frames are captured at evenly spaced intervals. Google Meet may vary the time between frames based on network and performance conditions, resulting in a variable-frame-rate video file.

## Detect if a video has a constant of variable frame rate

Use the vfrcheck.sh script to find out if a file has constant of variable framerate. (It uses ffmpeg to do the check).
You can also pass in a directory name for it to recursively check all video files within that directory.

# Convert video to constant frame rate

Convert the video to a constant-frame-rate before importing it into Blender.

```bash
ffmpeg -i input.mp4 -fps_mode cfr -r 24 -c:v libx264 -crf 18 -preset fast -c:a aac -b:a 192k output_cfr.mp4
```

## Extract the shared screen and participants videos into two separate files

Use the crop2.sh script to extract the rectangle of the shared desktop screen, and the rectable of the participants video into two separate video files.

```bash
./crop2.sh google_meet.mp4 
```

Use the to1080p.sh script to convert the shared desktop screen video into 1080p resolution.

```bash
./to1080p.sh desktop_screen.mp4 desktop_screen_1080p.mp4
```

